WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
HEATHER: I think this collaboration is&nbsp;
really important because it challenges us.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.320
QINGLIN: Academic exchanges&nbsp;
expose you to different ideas.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.280
HONG: People just learn from each other.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.560
HEATHER: We've been able to have some&nbsp;
quite uncomfortable conversations.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.680
HONG: Because I came from&nbsp;
there so I know how they work.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.680
QINGLIN: Although we sometimes have different&nbsp;
opinions we are really good friends.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.400
HEATHER: I think collaboration is essential&nbsp;
to science and the greater diversity of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.400
we collaborate with then the greater the&nbsp;
benefits. I think it's as simple as that.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.320
HEATHER:&nbsp;I'm Heather Viles, I'm professor&nbsp;
of biogeomorphology and heritage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.240
conservation at the University of Oxford.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:50.240
QINGLIN: My name is Qinglin Guo. I&nbsp;
belong to Dunhuang Academy in China.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.800
HEATHER: Dunhuang Research Academy&nbsp;
is responsible for looking after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.040
some of the major heritage&nbsp;
sites in north west China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.560
and the team we work with are a group of&nbsp;
scientists led by professor Qinglin Guo.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.640
QINGLIN: Our research is mainly focused on the&nbsp;
conservation and preservation of cultural heritage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.920
and studies the destruction&nbsp;
of sites by wind and rain.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.080
HEATHER: And we're trying to investigate&nbsp;
whether planting things around them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.840
or enhancing the natural vegetation, can help&nbsp;
protect them against further deterioration.

00:01:29.396 --> 00:01:32.840
JENNY: So these blocks have been made&nbsp;
by the Dunhuang Academy in China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:39.840
We use this 3D microscope to look at the surface&nbsp;
of the blocks to see what happens over time.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.720
HONG: So this afternoon I'm going&nbsp;
to use the machine to measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.160
ultrasonic pulse velocity on my earthen cubes.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.000
HEATHER: I'm very lucky that&nbsp;
within my group I have Hong Zhang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.040
who is Chinese and has very good&nbsp;
connections with Chinese colleagues.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:06.720
HONG: I'm a translator, I'm a coordinator&nbsp;
and also I'm a researcher. We started this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:15.520
collaboration actually from 2015 because I've got&nbsp;
quite a lot of friends who are working in China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:21.840
I said to my friends, I said look, obviously&nbsp;
you have those amazing heritage sites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:29.920
but my boss, Professor Heather Viles, I think she&nbsp;
started this kind of concept. For example like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.960
learn from nature, learn from the past. So&nbsp;
I thought why can't we just work together.

00:02:37.845 --> 00:02:43.760
QINGLIN: Our collaboration has lasted for&nbsp;
almost 10 years you know. We started from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.840
several international meetings. We then&nbsp;
began to exchange our researcher[s].

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.080
HEATHER: So we talk reasonably&nbsp;
often but at the moment we talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.360
mainly through Hong. So sometimes Hong, Jenny and&nbsp;
I will be in a meeting and we'll be discussing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.040
you know, something to do with the experiment&nbsp;
and I will say, you know off the top of my head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.960
oh Hong could you ask our Dunhuang colleagues&nbsp;
about this? She will almost instantaneously get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:17.840
onto her phone get onto WeChat and, within often&nbsp;
half an hour, we have an answer to the question.

00:03:18.423 --> 00:03:21.920
JENNY: We're looking at the&nbsp;
very surface of the earthen cube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.680
and we can see the bits that have been&nbsp;
subjected to the harshest conditions have become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.840
far more rough, whereas at the&nbsp;
start it was very very smooth.

00:03:32.164 --> 00:03:36.139
INTERVIEWER: Is the culture of doing&nbsp;
science different between the UK and China?

00:03:36.139 --> 00:03:39.120
HEATHER: I believe there are&nbsp;
quite significant differences yes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.960
Some of those I think are generational, so&nbsp;
I think if, if you were a scientist in the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:50.640
starting off in the 70s and 80s in&nbsp;
China, then their systems were quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.200
isolated from the West and I think&nbsp;
they had very specific viewpoints.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.320
QINGLIN: I think there are some&nbsp;
differences really especially the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:08.800
of thinking [about] scientific problems. I feel science in the UK is more like philosophy.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.840
Chinese scientists are more focused&nbsp;
on solving practical problems.

00:04:14.401 --> 00:04:16.160
HEATHER: And that's quite unusual.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:22.425
In Britain the science is often quite a&nbsp;
long way away from the the practicalities.

00:04:22.425 --> 00:04:29.600
QINGLIN: Another huge difference between [the] two&nbsp;
teams is is the way of expression. You know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.040
Western people always dare to express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:39.360
their opinions, while our culture tells&nbsp;
us we should think more before we talk.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.520
HEATHER: The really obvious cultural&nbsp;
difference that sticks in my mind is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:48.800
the difference between work and home.&nbsp;
I think they found it rather strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.280
when they came as individuals to spend&nbsp;
time here for a year and they think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:58.160
I think they felt that I was rather cold&nbsp;
because I would come to meetings with people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.760
but I wouldn't invite them round to my house.&nbsp;
And you know I like to have some down time.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.440
JENNY: So after dinner was like,&nbsp;
right, so you've got your meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.028
to work out what's happening tomorrow,&nbsp;
and I was like Hong... it's half ten!

00:05:10.028 --> 00:05:16.240
HONG: Yeah that that's why, you see, why&nbsp;
I said that to Jenny, probably I feel a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:22.800
little bit sorry because I know - for jenny&nbsp;
who's growing up here - uh, probably wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:30.800
like, do that kind of things, after, outside&nbsp;
working hours. But that is very normal in China.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.920
INTERVIEWER: Are there any more&nbsp;
examples of things that you have to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.240
Jenny or Heather or, you&nbsp;
know, English researchers?

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:37.840
HONG: Um...

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:40.557
JENNY: When they told me to sit in the van&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.640
because it was too much work and&nbsp;
telling me not to get offended by it.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:51.840
HONG: Yeah, yeah yeah. And I mean in China&nbsp;
for carrying out those field work... mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:06:02.640
just... men. She already noticed. When Jenny&nbsp;
was there those Chinese guys they thought oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:12.560
she's a student firstly, secondly she's a girl&nbsp;
- and you know under this very hot weather it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:21.440
too, too tough for for a lady. They just try to,&nbsp;
for example, like treat her like a sister. Try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:28.800
to protect her. But without realizing probably&nbsp;
that could be a problem in a Western country.

00:06:30.719 --> 00:06:35.440
JENNY: This is an environmental cabinet&nbsp;
and it's used to simulate environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:39.600
conditions. So we're simulating&nbsp;
what happens in winter so when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.480
it's really cold during the night&nbsp;
and then warms up during the day.

00:06:46.479 --> 00:06:53.200
HONG: Chinese people are very...&nbsp;
hospitality. Each time, we don't need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:07:02.800
pay, like, food, accommodation. I think after so&nbsp;
many years, Heather already changed something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:09.680
So when they, when Chinese friends, I mean&nbsp;
Chinese colleagues, come here, each time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:20.560
Heather tries to organise a nice meal for&nbsp;
them. Just to show her, you know, appreciation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:28.480
So I think somehow by the collaboration, I&nbsp;
mean people just uh, learn from each other.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:32.320
HONG: So my english is not&nbsp;
very good, so you have to...

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.120
JENNY: Hong did you just say&nbsp;
your english is not very good?

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.364
HONG: Yeah, not not good enough! (laughs)

00:07:38.364 --> 00:07:39.649
JENNY: Hong!

00:07:40.253 --> 00:07:44.720
QINGLIN: Many challenges and&nbsp;
difficulties occur in such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:53.840
transnational teamwork, such as differences&nbsp;
in language, time lag, culture. However they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:00.400
[are] differences we can overcome compared&nbsp;
with the benefits from our collaboration.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.960
HEATHER: One of the areas that we found to&nbsp;
be quite challenging is deciding whose name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:11.120
gets onto the paper that comes out as a result&nbsp;
of the experiments we're doing, and what order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:16.000
the names are in. And what we've discovered&nbsp;
is that Chinese practice is quite different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.760
to British practice. And I think it's a&nbsp;
measure of the success of our collaboration that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:27.040
we've been able to have some quite uncomfortable&nbsp;
conversations, and come up with something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:32.720
works at the international agreed&nbsp;
level of, kind of, research ethics.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:40.960
QINGLIN: Although we sometimes have different&nbsp;
opinions we can research consensus quickly because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:53.840
we have the same goal, which is to protect the&nbsp;
cultural heritages which belong to all of mankind.

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:59.440
INTERVIEWER: The big sort of, political goings-on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.560
how do you deal with the fact that they're&nbsp;
there? And you do talk about them at all?

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:09.760
HEATHER: Um, that's a really good question. I mean&nbsp;
politics is always just very narrowly under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:16.240
surface. My experience is that it's really hard to&nbsp;
talk to Chinese colleagues in any meaningful way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:24.080
about these things. Even when they're in Britain&nbsp;
they're quite reticent about being, being open and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.800
it's quite interesting for them to be sat in&nbsp;
our lab group when we're having some political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.320
argument and you can tell they feel quite&nbsp;
uncomfortable. And I think when we go to China we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:41.280
also feel uncomfortable because, you know, we see&nbsp;
all the cameras taking pictures of every vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:47.760
as you go along motorways. And I think each&nbsp;
one of us as individuals has to navigate that.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:53.200
HONG: We are comparing P... sorry I said 'P wave'&nbsp;again.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:58.600
Because we are comparing&nbsp;P...

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:01.840
(laughs) ...sorry!

00:10:03.765 --> 00:10:08.880
HEATHER: I think there's a real challenge in&nbsp;
trying to actually get to know and understand each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:16.240
other, and the way that we've really, I think,&nbsp;
got round that challenge is through spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:24.960
as much time together as we, as we can. And also&nbsp;
luckily perhaps sharing a good sense of humour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:29.280
And that would be my tip for&nbsp;
any collaboration actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:34.880
is that if you can laugh together you can&nbsp;
probably start to really understand each other.

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:42.000
QINGLIN: One of the basic prerequisites is&nbsp;
trust. When you have doubts you can ask questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:49.920
but do not rush to deny the other&nbsp;
team before you fully understand.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.200
HEATHER: If I was going to advise&nbsp;
colleagues who are wanting to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.982
out on these collaborations I would say be humble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:01.040
I think one of the dangers that I've learned - and&nbsp;
this is through collaborating with people anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:07.520
in the world - is do not spout what you think&nbsp;
is something really sensible in the first five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:14.400
minutes. Always listening to people, trying&nbsp;
to be humble, and say 'I don't really know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:21.040
what - what do you think?' is a much better way of,&nbsp;
of, of getting a shared understanding of things.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:25.760
HEATHER:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:31.440
Perhaps the best benefit that I've had from this&nbsp;
collaboration is when I go to China and I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:37.920
to give a lecture, and it has to be translated&nbsp;
slide by slide into Chinese, and it takes forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:43.360
and it's really stressful, and at the end&nbsp;
I get so many really interesting questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:47.440
and I've just been exposed to a lot&nbsp;
of different ideas and viewpoints.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:51.120
QINGLIN: You know Heather&nbsp;
is very clever and so kind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:57.840
It's very relaxed [to] stay with her and her&nbsp;
teams. She's always able to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:04.560
herself in your shoes. She's my really&nbsp;
friend during our collaboration.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:08.000
HEATHER: I've absolutely loved&nbsp;
collaborating with them. I mean they've,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:13.280
they've shown me sights that I would never&nbsp;
have seen otherwise. They've just given me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:19.960
experience to work with different people in&nbsp;
in different ways. I've really enjoyed it.

00:12:21.104 --> 00:12:30.080
HONG: For me I think, firstly, even I'm working&nbsp;
here, I can also contribute to those historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.990
sites in China. So this is a kind of, you&nbsp;
know the feeling...

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:41.042
You feel very happy about it.

